<h2 id="section1">1. Introduction and Domain Model</h2>
<p>
For my project in the <strong>Automated Software Testing</strong> course, I developed a desktop application in <strong>Java 17</strong> using <strong>Swing</strong> for managing event ticketing and venue seat reservations, with persistence handled by a <strong>MySQL 8.0</strong> relational database.
</p>
<p>
The main objective of this project was not to build an overly complex domain, but rather to apply all the testing methodologies and quality practices taught during the course: Test-Driven Development (TDD), the testing pyramid (unit, integration, and end-to-end GUI tests), 100% code coverage, mutation testing with zero surviving mutants, zero third-party mocking, clean code verified with SonarCloud, and build automation using Maven and GitHub Actions.
</p>

<h3>My Domain Model</h3>
<p>
I designed four core domain classes inside package <code>com.ticketreservation.model</code>:
</p>
<ul>
    <li><strong><code>User</code>:</strong> Represents the person logging into the application. Each user has an ID, username, password, active flag, and a role (<code>CUSTOMER</code> or <code>ORGANIZER</code>).</li>
    <li><strong><code>Event</code>:</strong> Represents an event taking place at a venue. It stores the title, date, venue location, total seats, and the number of currently available seats.</li>
    <li><strong><code>Seat</code>:</strong> Represents a specific seat for an event (e.g. <code>VIP-1</code> or <code>REG-10</code>). It has a section (<code>VIP</code> or <code>REGULAR</code>), a price, and a status (<code>AVAILABLE</code>, <code>RESERVED</code>, or <code>BOOKED</code>).</li>
    <li><strong><code>TicketReservation</code>:</strong> Represents a confirmed booking connecting a <code>User</code>, an <code>Event</code>, and a <code>Seat</code>, along with the timestamp of the reservation and its active status.</li>
</ul>

