<h2 id="section2">2. Architecture and Design Choices</h2>
<p>
I organized the codebase following a <strong>Three-Tier Architecture</strong> and the <strong>Model-View-Controller (MVC)</strong> pattern to ensure strict separation of concerns and high testability:
</p>
<ul>
    <li><strong>View Layer (<code>com.ticketreservation.view</code>):</strong> Built with Java Swing (<code>LoginView</code>, <code>EventSwingView</code>, <code>AdminView</code>). The views are responsible only for presentation and user input. They delegate all user actions to the controllers and never execute SQL queries directly.</li>
    <li><strong>Controller Layer (<code>com.ticketreservation.controller</code>):</strong> Contains the business logic (<code>UserController</code>, <code>EventController</code>, <code>TicketController</code>). The controllers validate inputs (e.g., rejecting negative seat numbers, empty usernames, invalid dates, or attempts to book sold-out events) and coordinate persistence via repository interfaces.</li>
    <li><strong>Repository Layer (<code>com.ticketreservation.repository</code>):</strong> Defines pure Java interfaces (<code>UserRepository</code>, <code>EventRepository</code>, <code>TicketRepository</code>) and their concrete MySQL JDBC implementations (<code>MysqlUserRepository</code>, etc.).</li>
</ul>

<div class="callout">
    <div class="callout-title">Adherence to Course Rule: Zero Third-Party Mocking</div>
    A key guideline from the course is that <strong>third-party code must never be mocked</strong>, because mocking types we do not own (such as JDBC <code>Connection</code>, <code>PreparedStatement</code>, <code>ResultSet</code>, <code>DataSource</code>, or Swing's <code>JFileChooser</code>) produces brittle tests and false assumptions about external behavior.<br><br>
    To strictly follow this rule:
    <ul>
        <li>In my repository tests, I never mocked JDBC interfaces. Instead, I used <strong>Testcontainers</strong> to run integration tests against a real MySQL database.</li>
        <li>In my view tests, I needed to test the "Export to PDF" button, which opens a file save dialog. Rather than mocking <code>JFileChooser</code> (which violates course rules), I created my own interface: <code>FileSaveDialog</code>. In production, <code>DefaultFileSaveDialog</code> wraps <code>JFileChooser</code>. In my tests for <code>EventSwingView</code>, I mock only <code>FileSaveDialog</code>&mdash;an interface that I own.</li>
    </ul>
</div>

