<h2 id="section3">3. Technologies Used &amp; Detailed Explanations</h2>

<h3>3.1 MySQL Atomic Transactions in Ticket Reservation</h3>
<p>
When multiple customers book seats simultaneously, race conditions can occur (such as two users booking the same seat or decrementing available seats below zero). To ensure data consistency and satisfy ACID requirements, I implemented atomic database transactions using JDBC:
</p>
<p>
In <code>MysqlTicketRepository.java</code>, the booking operation requires four coordinated steps:
</p>
<ol>
    <li>Verifying that the target seat currently has status <code>AVAILABLE</code>.</li>
    <li>Inserting the new record into the <code>ticket_reservations</code> table.</li>
    <li>Updating the seat status in the <code>seats</code> table to <code>RESERVED</code>.</li>
    <li>Decrementing the <code>available_seats</code> count in the <code>events</code> table.</li>
</ol>
<p>
I handled this transaction explicitly:
</p>
<ul>
    <li>I call <code>connection.setAutoCommit(false)</code> before running the queries.</li>
    <li>If all statements execute successfully, I call <code>connection.commit()</code>.</li>
    <li>If any <code>SQLException</code> is thrown at any step, the <code>catch</code> block immediately calls <code>connection.rollback()</code>, ensuring the database reverts to its original state and no half-completed bookings or orphaned records remain.</li>
    <li>In the <code>finally</code> block, I restore <code>connection.setAutoCommit(true)</code>.</li>
</ul>

<h3>3.2 Testcontainers for Real Database Testing</h3>
<p>
Instead of using in-memory databases like H2 (which do not support the exact same SQL syntax, locking mechanisms, or transaction isolation as MySQL), I used <strong>Testcontainers</strong>:
</p>
<ul>
    <li>For every integration test run (<code>*IT.java</code>), Testcontainers automatically pulls and starts an official <code>mysql:8.0</code> Docker container on a random free port.</li>
    <li>It applies the database schema from <code>src/main/resources/db/init.sql</code>.</li>
    <li>This allowed me to test real SQL queries, foreign key constraints, and transaction rollbacks against an actual MySQL server, both locally and inside GitHub Actions CI.</li>
</ul>

