</div>

<h3>3.3 Automated GUI Testing with AssertJ-Swing</h3>
<p>
Testing desktop Swing interfaces can be tricky because GUI components must be updated on Swing's Event Dispatch Thread (EDT). To write deterministic, automated GUI tests:
</p>
<ul>
    <li>I used <strong>AssertJ-Swing</strong>. Every view creation and UI assertion runs safely on the EDT using <code>GuiActionRunner.execute(...)</code>.</li>
    <li>I assigned explicit names to UI components using <code>.setName()</code> (e.g. <code>bookButton</code>, <code>eventTable</code>, <code>errorLabel</code>), allowing <code>FrameFixture</code> to find components reliably.</li>
    <li>In my GitHub Actions CI pipeline on Ubuntu, I used <strong><code>xvfb-run</code></strong> (X Virtual Framebuffer) so that Swing windows can render in virtual memory without needing a physical monitor.</li>
</ul>

<h3>3.4 PDF Ticket Export with OpenPDF</h3>
<p>
To allow customers to download and print their tickets, I used <strong>OpenPDF</strong> (<code>com.github.librepdf:openpdf</code>):
</p>
<ul>
    <li>It generates a formatted PDF document containing the customer's name, event details, venue, seat number, price, booking reference, and timestamp.</li>
    <li>To keep the UI responsive, the PDF generation handles I/O exceptions gracefully and displays an error message on the view if the file cannot be written.</li>
</ul>

