<h2 id="section4">4. Testing Strategy and Testing Pyramid</h2>
<p>
I organized my test suite to respect the <strong>Testing Pyramid</strong>:
</p>

<table>
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th>Layer</th>
            <th>Runner &amp; Location</th>
            <th>Frameworks</th>
            <th>Purpose</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td><strong>Unit Tests</strong></td>
            <td><code>src/test/java/</code><br>Maven Surefire</td>
            <td>JUnit 5, AssertJ, Mockito</td>
            <td>Test individual business rules in controllers and models in isolation using Mockito mocks of my repository interfaces. Very fast execution.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><strong>Integration Tests</strong></td>
            <td><code>src/it/java/</code><br>Maven Failsafe</td>
            <td>Testcontainers, MySQL 8.0</td>
            <td>Test concrete repository classes against a real MySQL container, verifying SQL queries and transaction rollbacks.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><strong>End-to-End Tests</strong></td>
            <td><code>src/e2e/java/</code><br>Maven Failsafe</td>
            <td>AssertJ-Swing, Testcontainers</td>
            <td>Test the complete user workflow from login, viewing events, selecting seats, booking, and exporting to PDF.</td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

<h2 id="section5">5. Code Coverage (JaCoCo) and Exclusions</h2>
<p>
I used the <strong>JaCoCo Maven Plugin</strong> to track code coverage during the build:
</p>
<ul>
    <li><strong>Result:</strong> I achieved <strong>100% line coverage and 100% branch coverage</strong> across all business controllers, domain models, repositories, view presenters, and utilities.</li>
    <li><strong>Motivated Exclusions:</strong>
        <ul>
            <li><code>**/app/**</code>: I excluded <code>TicketApp.java</code>, which contains only the <code>main</code> method that parses command-line arguments and sets the Swing Look &amp; Feel.</li>
            <li><code>**/view/DefaultFileSaveDialog.java</code>: This is a thin 10-line adapter that simply delegates to Swing's <code>JFileChooser.showSaveDialog()</code>. Because native file choosers block the thread and cannot be tested headlessly without third-party mocking, I excluded this single wrapper class.</li>
        </ul>
    </li>
</ul>

