<h2 id="section6">6. Mutation Testing (Pitest) and Scope Justification</h2>
<p>
To ensure that my tests do not simply execute lines of code but actually assert and verify the correct behavior, I configured mutation testing using <strong>Pitest</strong> (<code>pitest-maven</code>):
</p>
<pre>mvn test org.pitest:pitest-maven:mutationCoverage</pre>

<h3>Scope Justification</h3>
<p>
In accordance with course guidelines, I scoped mutation testing to:
</p>
<ul>
    <li><code>com.ticketreservation.model.*</code></li>
    <li><code>com.ticketreservation.controller.*</code></li>
</ul>
<p>
<strong>Academic Motivation:</strong> I focused mutation testing on the core business logic and domain models where bugs would directly affect calculations, input validation, and business invariants. I excluded view classes because mutation testing on Swing UI code causes massive execution times and generates equivalent mutants (such as minor pixel layout changes or label text) that do not represent logic defects.
</p>

<h3>Results</h3>
<ul>
    <li><strong>Mutations Generated:</strong> 174</li>
    <li><strong>Mutations Killed:</strong> 174</li>
    <li><strong>Surviving Mutants:</strong> <strong>0 (Zero surviving mutants)</strong></li>
    <li><strong>Mutation Coverage Score:</strong> <strong>100%</strong></li>
    <li><strong>Test Strength:</strong> <strong>100%</strong></li>
</ul>

