<h2 id="section7">7. Static Analysis with SonarCloud and Motivated Rule Exclusions</h2>
<p>
I used <strong>SonarCloud</strong> to continuously analyze code quality and ensure the project maintains zero technical debt:
</p>
<ul>
    <li><strong>Bugs:</strong> 0</li>
    <li><strong>Vulnerabilities:</strong> 0</li>
    <li><strong>Security Hotspots:</strong> 0</li>
    <li><strong>Code Smells:</strong> 0</li>
    <li><strong>Technical Debt:</strong> 0 minutes</li>
    <li><strong>Duplicated Lines:</strong> 0.0%</li>
</ul>

<h3>Motivated Rule Exclusions in <code>pom.xml</code></h3>
<p>
As permitted by the course guidelines when supported by clear technical reasoning, I configured four exclusions:
</p>
<ol>
    <li><strong><code>java:S2699</code> (Tests should contain assertions) on <code>**/*Test.java</code>:</strong> AssertJ-Swing GUI tests use robot fixture verifications (e.g., <code>window.label("errorLabel").requireText(...)</code>, <code>window.button("bookButton").requireEnabled()</code>) rather than standard JUnit <code>assertEquals()</code>. SonarCloud does not recognize AssertJ-Swing assertions, falsely reporting missing assertions on valid GUI tests.</li>
    <li><strong><code>java:S8924</code> (Mockito static imports) on <code>**/*Test.java</code>:</strong> Permitted Mockito static import conventions used throughout course examples.</li>
    <li><strong><code>java:S1192</code> (Define a constant instead of duplicating string literals) on <code>**/*Test.java</code>:</strong> In test code, repeating test values (like <code>"Opera Show"</code> or <code>"CUSTOMER"</code>) makes each test method independent, self-contained, and readable (following the DAMP test design principle over DRY).</li>
    <li><strong><code>java:S1450</code> (Keep fields local) on <code>**/view/**</code>:</strong> Swing components are kept as class fields so that AssertJ-Swing can find components by name and to ensure proper window disposal.</li>
</ol>

